The clinical vignette describes the sequential use of valproate, topiramate, and lamotrigine, culminating in Stevens-Johnson syndrome/toxic epidermal necrolysis. The primary consensus identifies the initiating pharmacokinetic event as the inhibition of glucuronidation (specifically via UDP-glucuronosyltransferase UGT1A4/UGT2B7) of lamotrigine by valproate. An alternative perspective frames the initiating step as cytochrome P450–mediated epoxidation/oxidation into reactive arene oxide intermediates. The core answer remains glucuronidation (or its inhibition), with epoxidation noted as the downstream bioactivation mechanism.
}

